{
  "B_expression": "-1/(2*(1+u)*((1+u)+sqrt((1+u)**2+r))**2)",
  "C_expression": "-1/(2*(1+u))",
  "derivation": "Revised to a u-dependent exact-root body. Set a=1+u (=z) and s=sqrt(a^2+r). In the full inner support where chi_r*chi_u=1, the increment is g = r/2 + r^2 B + u r C. With C=-1/(2a), the first-order r part becomes r/2 + u r C = r/(2a). With B=-1/(2a(a+s)^2), the increment is g = r/(2a) - r^2/(2a(a+s)^2). Since (a+s)^2 - r = 2a(a+s), this equals r/(a+s) = s-a. Therefore z+g = a+s = s = sqrt(a^2+r) = sqrt(p), so the whole residual (z+g)^2-p is zero, up to floating-point error, wherever the product of the chi factors is one. At u=0, a=1 and B reduces to the previous source body -1/(2*(1+sqrt(1+r))^2), while C is inactive; source compatibility is retained. The actual feedback shows O(ur) whole residuals for the previous C=0 program (for example UNIFORM x=1/8 s=0: residual about -2.04e-8 with u about 7.88e-3 and r about -2.59e-6); the revision is motivated by canceling that whole-residual cross term locally. Smoothness: on the outer rectangle a in [3/4,5/4] and a^2+r >= (3/4)^2-1/2 = 1/16 >0, so the square root and all denominators are positive and C-infinity on an open neighborhood. In transition regions the chi factors blend the correction; outside the outer rectangle the host fallback r/2 applies. This is a local scalar coefficient identity, not a PDE estimate, global stability result, or NS proof.",
  "inspect": [],
  "note": "Active program is revised, not retained. Successor: (1) In full support the algebra makes the whole residual vanish; any nonzero residual should be roundoff or transition/fallback. (2) Out-of-support rows with |u|>=1/4 (e.g. LEFT x=1/8 s=0 with u about -0.625) receive the fallback linear increment by design; large residuals there are not failures of the smooth body. (3) Domain safety for this body is a^2+r>0; the evaluation rectangle has margin 1/16. If bodies are ever evaluated outside the rectangle, verify that margin; a complete program outside uses r/2. (4) The C slot is now nonzero and couples the r and u slots; use archived D0/D1 if a source-only u=0 comparison is needed. (5) No formal analytic certificate is claimed beyond the explicit positivity/smoothness argument; the null certificate in feedback does not change the local algebra. (6) ZERO rows have r=0,u=0, g=0, and remain unchanged."
}